                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]TL;DR at Bottom[/h1]\n[b]If you enjoyed this review,consider giving it a thumbs up![/b]\n\n[h1]Never Purchase[/h1]\n\n[h1]A Broken Cyborg in a Broken Game[/h1]\n\nHeavily Armed is a game which I received without cost, for reviewing purposes, and even still would not recommend under any circumstances. After received the code and began the install, I decided to bide my time by checking out the games Steam Page, seeing the screenshots and watching the trailer; all of this leaving me cautious but optimistic. The screenshots are all incredibly vague, showing only their best environments and not much else. The reason for this being that the game looks half decent when you're only looking at their best still shots, but when you actually get into the game and begin playing you find that it looks like it was modeled in about an hour, and incredibly poorly as well. The guns seem to vary in that one or two of them have some actually decent models, but the rest are all barely even modeled to begin with. Poor modeling aside, the weapons are just plain boring as well and follow typical archetypes for weapons in a shooting game. You have your assault rifle, which can barely hit anything at all, your shotgun, and your two types of pistol: normal and suppressed. All of these weapons feel absolutely terrible, with just about no recoil they feel as if you're wielding nothing at all. All of the weapons both look and feel pathetic, which I could overlook if the game had other features going for it. Sadly, it has absolutely nothing at all.\n\nHeavily Armed does indeed have a story, about a cyborg who loses his memory after being shot by an unknown assailant that he has named \"Mister X\". I didn't get much further than the first level because the game both played and ran so horribly that I couldn't stand to play it for a second more. The story is told much in the same way as it is in games like Hard Reset, where bits of story are delivered to you at the start of each level in very comic book-esque panels. The story seems rather pathetic, though maybe it gets better as you go on, however much I may doubt that. The gameplay however, couldn't possibly manage to get any worse. Heavily Armed is optimized horribly and runs at a variable framerate that sometimes manages to reach 60-70fps, though for most of the game runs closer to about 20 or 30. On top of the game running horrendously, it's also filled with an atrocious amount of bugs that can sometimes make the game unplayable. Enemies can walk through walls, as well as shoot through them, and their animations are so bad I can never tell if they're bugging out or just that bad. You'll also be hard pressed to get the game to work, half the menu's don't actually work and all of this is assuming that you can get the game to run as it tends to crash or freeze up constantly.\n\n[h1]TL;DR[/h1]\nHeavily Armed is an ugly excuse for a third person shooter that has precious little going for it, and everything going against it.\n\n[h1]If you found this review helpful or entertaining then feel free to leave a comment and join my Steam Curator Group at  [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/9577715-Mudkips-Gaming/]Mudkips Gaming[/url]",
